    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "My paper aims to analyze the 2008 financial crisis, when Covered Interest Parity (CIP) between the U.S. dollar and the euro appears to have deviated by hundreds of basis points to historically unprecedented levels.  In existing literature, this deviation is attributed to heightened credit risk spreads and dollar liquidity premiums.  However, my paper shows that the Term Fed Funds explain the majority of these observed market deviations.  I thus argue that covered interest parity did not fail as an economic model, but rather that panel banks underreported their true borrowing rates in the London Inter-Bank Offered Rate (LIBOR) survey.  Ultimately I conclude that LIBOR, which is linked to more than $90 trillion of financial derivatives, broke down as an accurate measure of true interest rates during the 2008 financial crisis.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
